To enable your local or cloud agents (like Cline and Qwen) to read, analyze, and act upon all existing repositories on your server, you need to set up an Agent Orchestrator.
Instead of treating each repository as an isolated silo, the agent operates from a parent directory or connects via a centralized Discovery and Integration Layer.
Here is exactly how the agent engages with this function step-by-step:
------------------------------
## 1. The Directory and Workspace Mapping (How it Accesses Files)
For an agent to read multiple repositories, it must be granted a workspace context that sits above them, or use programmatic file system access.
## Option A: The Local Monorepo/Workspace Approach
If your agents run locally on your server, you point Cline's workspace or the agent's file system tool to a root folder containing all projects.

/development-server/
│
├── .agent-orchestrator/           <-- Where your main scripts & prompts live
│   ├── self-healing-loop.md
│   └── skills.md (Global Master Template)
│
├── remote-agent-repo-1/           <-- Target App 1 (Has its own active skills.md)
├── remote-agent-repo-2/           <-- Target App 2 (Has its own active skills.md)
└── remote-agent-repo-3/           <-- Target App 3 (Has its own active skills.md)


* Execution: You pass a command to Cline specifying the target subdirectory: cline --workspace /development-server/ --instruction "Run self-healing on remote-agent-repo-1".

## Option B: The Cloud / API-Driven Approach
If your agent is running in the cloud, it cannot see your local drive directly. It accesses your repositories via a Local Agent Gateway or Git APIs.

   1. The cloud agent sends a request to your server's secure API wrapper.
   2. The server runs a local indexing script (find . -name "package.json" or checking Git configurations).
   3. The server sends back a JSON tree of all available repositories to the cloud agent.

------------------------------
## 2. The Step-by-Step Engagement Flow
When you tell the agent to run the self-healing function across your repos, it follows this automated loop:

[Agent Starts] ➔ [1. Discover Repos] ➔ [2. Read Target skills.md] ➔ [3. Boot App & Run Playwright] ➔ [4. Self-Heal Loop]

## Step 1: Repository Discovery
The agent executes a discovery command using its terminal tool to list all project directories and check their status:

# The agent checks which repositories are available and if they contain a design genome
find . -maxdepth 2 -name "skills.md"

## Step 2: Context Loading
The agent moves inside the targeted repository (e.g., remote-agent-repo-1). It reads the local skills.md file to memorize the exact design tokens (colors, fonts, glassmorphism blur settings) assigned to that specific web application.
## Step 3: Headless Port Allocation & Simulation
Because the agent needs to test the app locally, your orchestrator must prevent port conflicts if multiple agents are working at the same time:

   1. The agent dynamically finds an open port (e.g., 3001).
   2. It starts the local development server: PORT=3001 npm run dev.
   3. It launches Playwright in headless mode, pointing it directly at http://localhost:3001.
   4. Playwright interacts with the app, generating screenshots, DOM extractions, and console logs, saving them into a temporary folder (/.stress_reports/).

## Step 4: Visual & Logical Audit (Qwen-Vision Engagement)
The local or cloud agent passes the generated screenshots and runtime error logs to its vision model engine. It checks if the live components match the instructions found in that specific repository's skills.md.
## Step 5: Direct File Modification (The Self-Healing Action)
If Qwen-Vision flags a layout rupture (e.g., a card component text overflowing on mobile screen sizes):

   1. The agent searches the directory for the component file using regex or tools (e.g., grep -r "CardComponent" ./src).
   2. It opens the exact file and rewrites the broken code block.
   3. It saves the file, allowing the local server to hot-reload.
   4. It commands Playwright to re-run the test suite.

This process repeats inside that specific repository until the test results yield zero errors. Once finished, the agent checks out, commits the fixes via Git, and moves to the next repository in the server tree.
